  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 128 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThe persistent trof over the eastern CONUS will start to break down \nthrough the first half of the work week, with the ridge that has \nbeen locked in place overhead finally starting to track easterly. As \nthis shifts east, upper level flow becomes southwesterly as a trof \ndigs into the southwestern CONUS. This long fetch of southwesterly \nflow will have a connection to the Eastern Pacific, where we \nhave tropical systems in place. This pattern will help guide \nthese systems northeast into the central CONUS, serving as a \nmoisture conveyor into the region. Not only will this help \nsupply moisture to the region, but llvl southerly/southwesterly \nflow will usher Gulf moisture in as well. Thus, an anomalously \nmoist airmass will be in place through mid week, with PWATS \nbetween 1.50-2.00\" moving in. \n\nThe wave over the Rockies will also guide bouts of energy into the \nUpper Mississippi River Valley, with a baroclinic zone setting up \nnearby and serving as a focal point for precipitation chances. Thus, \nwith plenty of moisture and forcing moving into the area, \nprecipitation chances will be on the rise again. The first round \nof showers and storms may work into parts of the area as early \nas Tuesday night, with persistent PoPs remaining through \nWednesday and Thursday. There remains much uncertainty with the \nindividual storm tracks and timing though, with the location of \nthe baroclinic zone also uncertain. Thus, will largely message \nthat we will see chances for showers/storms through at least the\nmiddle of next week, with the potential for heavy rainfall as \nwell. As such, the WPC has highlighted our area in a Day 4/5 \nSlight Risk, level 2 of 4, for Excessive Rainfall and Flash \nFlooding. Stay tuned on upcoming forecasts for updates on \ntiming and potential amounts as confidence increases. Late week \nand into the weekend, guidance favors a continuation of the \nactive weather, with PoPs persisting.",
